{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Quick adds deny by default for custom permissions - AWS","context":"Amazon Quick, a service provided by AWS, has introduced a new governance setting called \"deny by default\" for custom permissions. Previously, new AI capabilities in Amazon Quick were available to all users upon release, necessitating administrators to react after the fact. With deny by default, administrators can restrict new AI capability categories in a custom permissions profile and assign it to users, roles, or the entire account. Amazon Quick then denies any new AI capability at launch for those users, and the restriction also applies to existing capabilities within the restricted category. Administrators can explicitly allow each capability when they are ready. The restriction applies only to the profile configured and is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Quick is available","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/amazon-quick-deny-by-default/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon Quick adds deny by default for custom permissions - AWS","context":"Amazon Quick, a service provided by AWS, has introduced a new governance setting called \"deny by default\" for custom permissions. Previously, new AI capabilities in Amazon Quick were available to all users upon release, necessitating administrators to react after the fact. With deny by default, administrators can restrict new AI capability categories in a custom permissions profile and assign it to users, roles, or the entire account. Amazon Quick then denies any new AI capability at launch for those users. Restriction of a category also applies to existing capabilities within it. Administrators can explicitly allow each capability when they are ready. This setting can be configured in Manage account in Amazon Quick or through the AWS CLI. Deny by default is available in all AWS Regions where Amazon Quick is available","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/202